(2561795, 348596, 37960, '<td valign="top">Looks like the work of the great Ansel Adams/ Edward Weston. Great pic</td>', 'Looks like the work of the great Ansel Adams/ Edward Weston. Great pic', '2006-06-28 12:03:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2562357, 349210, 52713, '<td valign="top">---Greetings from the Critique Club!---<br/> <br/>First impression - Great shot!<br/> <br/>What can I say that hasn''t already been said by all the commenters? You really hit the mark on this one. Meets the challenge perfectly, great colors, nice detail in the sky, on and on.<br/> <br/>Since this is the critique club, the only element I would have done differently is the horizon. Horizons that cut right through the middle of the frame bother me a bit. If you look at great landscapes and seascapes you will see that the most powerful images have the horizon 1/3 form the bottom or 1/3 from the top. I think if you came up just a bit on the bottom and had a bit more sky this really strong image would be improved. Of course this is my opinion. I certainly think the shot is deserving of the high placement that the DPC voters gave.<br/> <br/>Congratulations on a superb capture!<br/> <br/>I hope this was helpful. Feel free to contact me if you have any questions.<br/> <br/>David<br/></td>', '---Greetings from the Critique Club!--- First impression - Great shot! What can I say that hasn''t already been said by all the commenters? You really hit the mark on this one. Meets the challenge perfectly, great colors, nice detail in the sky, on and on. Since this is the critique club, the only element I would have done differently is the horizon. Horizons that cut right through the middle of the frame bother me a bit. If you look at great landscapes and seascapes you will see that the most powerful images have the horizon 1/3 form the bottom or 1/3 from the top. I think if you came up just a bit on the bottom and had a bit more sky this really strong image would be improved. Of course this is my opinion. I certainly think the shot is deserving of the high placement that the DPC voters gave. Congratulations on a superb capture! I hope this was helpful. Feel free to contact me if you have any questions. David', '2006-06-28 14:29:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2562706, 349168, 23203, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club<br/> <br/>Congratulations! This is an emotive photograph that tells a story and draws the viewer into it.<br/> <br/>You''ve used the available light to great effect. As you mention, the noise adds to the effect. <br/> <br/>Cropping, composition, DoF are just perfect.<br/> <br/>Congrats again, and thanks for sharing.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club Congratulations! This is an emotive photograph that tells a story and draws the viewer into it. You''ve used the available light to great effect. As you mention, the noise adds to the effect.  Cropping, composition, DoF are just perfect. Congrats again, and thanks for sharing.', '2006-06-28 15:50:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(2562868, 347916, 50127, '<td valign="top">Awesome!!!<br/>Just looking through this challenge now and this is my favorite so far.  I pratically grew up on the Never-ending story and this photo encompasses that feeling perfectly!! I just wish there was no break through of light over on the right side where its all dark so that the feeling of the "terrible nothing" coming was stronger- but you cant control the clouds.<br/>Great photo, great title! Nice job :)</td>', 'Awesome!!!Just looking through this challenge now and this is my favorite so far.  I pratically grew up on the Never-ending story and this photo encompasses that feeling perfectly!! I just wish there was no break through of light over on the right side where its all dark so that the feeling of the "terrible nothing" coming was stronger- but you cant control the clouds.Great photo, great title! Nice job :)', '2006-06-28 16:25:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2563165, 349127, 52925, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I''m so happy to draw this image to critique; I simply love it. The composition is very strong and balanced, with the big tree on the right and the small figure on the left. The colors are wonderful. I especially love the washed out, bright grass, and the very moody, dark sky and tree. It almost looks more like a painting than a photo.<br/>The sky reminds me of the famous Dutch painters in history, like Vermeer and Rembrandt.<br/> <br/>If there had to be a point of critique I would think it was the expression on her face. It looks indeed more as if she is having fun than if she is crying for help, although from the challenge it is obvious what your intent was. I also would clone out the dark cloud just to the left of here.<br/> <br/>Apparently, not everyone agreed with me, but I still think a 6.29 is way too low a score. But then again, neither Rembrandt or Vermeer were particularly appreciated in their lifetime.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I''m so happy to draw this image to critique; I simply love it. The composition is very strong and balanced, with the big tree on the right and the small figure on the left. The colors are wonderful. I especially love the washed out, bright grass, and the very moody, dark sky and tree. It almost looks more like a painting than a photo.The sky reminds me of the famous Dutch painters in history, like Vermeer and Rembrandt. If there had to be a point of critique I would think it was the expression on her face. It looks indeed more as if she is having fun than if she is crying for help, although from the challenge it is obvious what your intent was. I also would clone out the dark cloud just to the left of here. Apparently, not everyone agreed with me, but I still think a 6.29 is way too low a score. But then again, neither Rembrandt or Vermeer were particularly appreciated in their lifetime.', '2006-06-28 17:51:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(2563260, 347147, 52439, '<td valign="top">Gorgeous shot, love the colours, love the ''framing'' of the tree and the light shining through the clouds.<br/>Really well shot, really effective.</td>', 'Gorgeous shot, love the colours, love the ''framing'' of the tree and the light shining through the clouds.Really well shot, really effective.', '2006-06-28 18:18:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2563616, 349533, 49652, '<td valign="top">This is so beautiful.  I didn''t vote this one, but from reading the comments, I guess you got hammered for DNMC.  I think it would have ribboned in Bokeh.  I am adding to my faves.</td>', 'This is so beautiful.  I didn''t vote this one, but from reading the comments, I guess you got hammered for DNMC.  I think it would have ribboned in Bokeh.  I am adding to my faves.', '2006-06-28 19:36:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(2563722, 349533, 8891, '<td valign="top">Thanks, Dawn! WAy too much spot editing for the Open Bokeh II challenge. And....back at you ;)!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-06-30 23:09:21.</i></td>', 'Thanks, Dawn! WAy too much spot editing for the Open Bokeh II challenge. And....back at you ;)!', '2006-06-28 19:52:45.000000', '2006-07-01 00:09:21.000000', 0),
(2564403, 348324, 47933, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club Dexter!<br/> <br/>At first glance I like the shot. The expression is great and Sierra is ceertainly a beautiful subject. The crop is nice as is the shutter speed used to show motion.<br/> <br/>My critiques would be just a couple of things. The blue cast is a bit too.....well.....blue. While it is lovely, I think a high contrast black and white or sepia might''ve worked a bit better for this type of image to show desolation. The other thing is that, while it is a nice picture, it doesn''t really scream desolation to me. I think her clothes and the way she was wearing them sorta hindered you from recieving the high votes needed to place in the top ten. The emblem on the shirt looks to be something "sporty" (it may not be but it does look that way) and how she has it unzipped seems to be more sultry than desolate. <br/> <br/>Overall a nice image that Sierra should be proud to hang on her wall.<br/> <br/>Thanks for the opportunity to critique this shot Dexter.<br/> <br/>Cheers - Phil<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Dexter! At first glance I like the shot. The expression is great and Sierra is ceertainly a beautiful subject. The crop is nice as is the shutter speed used to show motion. My critiques would be just a couple of things. The blue cast is a bit too.....well.....blue. While it is lovely, I think a high contrast black and white or sepia might''ve worked a bit better for this type of image to show desolation. The other thing is that, while it is a nice picture, it doesn''t really scream desolation to me. I think her clothes and the way she was wearing them sorta hindered you from recieving the high votes needed to place in the top ten. The emblem on the shirt looks to be something "sporty" (it may not be but it does look that way) and how she has it unzipped seems to be more sultry than desolate.  Overall a nice image that Sierra should be proud to hang on her wall. Thanks for the opportunity to critique this shot Dexter. Cheers - Phil', '2006-06-28 23:48:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2564623, 347421, 52713, '<td valign="top">---Greetings from the Critique Club!----<br/> <br/>First impression - Interesting use of contrast and negative space.<br/> <br/>I kinda like this shot, but I can see how the voters missed it. The lack of any real focal point makes it difficult to see the desolation that the photographer was seeing, feeling at the moment.<br/> <br/>The over processing along with the bright daylight conveys a sense of heat. Don''t know if that is what you were going for, but that is what I get out of it.<br/> <br/>I like the low angle. I wish there was just a bit more in the negative space area to convey more desolation. I''m not second guessing the artistic vision here, I''m just looking for something to improve the over all impact.<br/> <br/>You are obvioulsy more of an artist with a camera than a photographer shooting art. I applaud that but it probably won''t get you any high scores. I doubt that that matters much to you.<br/> <br/>Keep shooting what you feel.<br/> <br/>I hope this was helpful. Feel free to contact me if you have any questions.<br/> <br/>David<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-06-28 23:46:30.</i></td>', '---Greetings from the Critique Club!---- First impression - Interesting use of contrast and negative space. I kinda like this shot, but I can see how the voters missed it. The lack of any real focal point makes it difficult to see the desolation that the photographer was seeing, feeling at the moment. The over processing along with the bright daylight conveys a sense of heat. Don''t know if that is what you were going for, but that is what I get out of it. I like the low angle. I wish there was just a bit more in the negative space area to convey more desolation. I''m not second guessing the artistic vision here, I''m just looking for something to improve the over all impact. You are obvioulsy more of an artist with a camera than a photographer shooting art. I applaud that but it probably won''t get you any high scores. I doubt that that matters much to you. Keep shooting what you feel. I hope this was helpful. Feel free to contact me if you have any questions. David', '2006-06-29 00:46:02.000000', '2006-06-29 00:46:30.000000', 0),
(2564711, 348828, 19978, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>by strangeghost<br/> <br/><i>The first three parts of this critique are written based purely on examination of your photo. "Final thoughts" is written after reviewing your score, photographer''s comments, and voter comments.</i> <br/> <br/><b>TECHNIQUE</b> <br/>Subject is sharply focused. The many foreground branches are less so but this is not a problem and adds some depth to the image. Color range seems good. The sky is a satisfying blue though there seems to be a little haloing around the branches on the upper right and left. Maybe a tad oversharp? The birdhouse is well lit in what looks like high sun. Technically not a bad image other than the possible overprocessing on sharpness.<br/> <br/><b>COMPOSITION</b> <br/>I can see why you were attracted to this subject for this challenge. The heavily vined tree is intriguing and the bird''s house seems strangely out of place and isolated. However, the incredible density of the vines makes for a "messy" feel to the photo, and even the birdhouse subject is somewhat hidden behind them. Since the birdhouse is clearly the subject of the photo, you probably should have gotten closer and made more of an attempt to fill the frame with the birdhouse and just allowed enough of the background to give it some context and feeling of desolation, and left out some of the cluttered feel that the tree as a whole gives. It''s also disturbing to have to look through the branches to see the bird house itself. Your depth of field is fairly deep, keeping nearly all the branches well focused. It would have been interesting to see a shallower depth, with the background falling softly out of focus. This would made it easier for the eye to find and rest on your intended subject. It''s also somewhat awkward that we''re looking only at the side of the birdhouse. I can''t tell if there''s an opening in the front of it or not. Your title suggests abandonment but it''s difficult to tell for sure with a side view.<br/> <br/><b>EMOTIONAL IMPACT</b> <br/>As shot, it''s a picture that looks a bit cluttered and seems to lack a clear voice. I think more attention paid to composition could have made this much stronger and given it an emotional "punch" that your audience would have found memorable. <br/> <br/><b>FINAL THOUGHTS</b> <br/>In general, your commenters seemed to zero in on the same things I mentioned. It does have a busy, cluttered feel to it. Your final score of 4.5 is below average and probably signifies a mostly quick appraisal, failure to connect immediately, so vote and move on. The fact that this birdhouse spoke to you indicates, to me, a good vision for a tough, conceptual challenge like this one. Good job on finding a worthy subject. Now really spend some time figuring out how to capture it in a way that adds drama, conflict, emotion, or purpose. Most of all, study composition. How can you make use of your subject, its textures and location, to make this image talk to people. Keep shooting, I like your style.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club by strangeghost The first three parts of this critique are written based purely on examination of your photo. "Final thoughts" is written after reviewing your score, photographer''s comments, and voter comments.  TECHNIQUE Subject is sharply focused. The many foreground branches are less so but this is not a problem and adds some depth to the image. Color range seems good. The sky is a satisfying blue though there seems to be a little haloing around the branches on the upper right and left. Maybe a tad oversharp? The birdhouse is well lit in what looks like high sun. Technically not a bad image other than the possible overprocessing on sharpness. COMPOSITION I can see why you were attracted to this subject for this challenge. The heavily vined tree is intriguing and the bird''s house seems strangely out of place and isolated. However, the incredible density of the vines makes for a "messy" feel to the photo, and even the birdhouse subject is somewhat hidden behind them. Since the birdhouse is clearly the subject of the photo, you probably should have gotten closer and made more of an attempt to fill the frame with the birdhouse and just allowed enough of the background to give it some context and feeling of desolation, and left out some of the cluttered feel that the tree as a whole gives. It''s also disturbing to have to look through the branches to see the bird house itself. Your depth of field is fairly deep, keeping nearly all the branches well focused. It would have been interesting to see a shallower depth, with the background falling softly out of focus. This would made it easier for the eye to find and rest on your intended subject. It''s also somewhat awkward that we''re looking only at the side of the birdhouse. I can''t tell if there''s an opening in the front of it or not. Your title suggests abandonment but it''s difficult to tell for sure with a side view. EMOTIONAL IMPACT As shot, it''s a picture that looks a bit cluttered and seems to lack a clear voice. I think more attention paid to composition could have made this much stronger and given it an emotional "punch" that your audience would have found memorable.  FINAL THOUGHTS In general, your commenters seemed to zero in on the same things I mentioned. It does have a busy, cluttered feel to it. Your final score of 4.5 is below average and probably signifies a mostly quick appraisal, failure to connect immediately, so vote and move on. The fact that this birdhouse spoke to you indicates, to me, a good vision for a tough, conceptual challenge like this one. Good job on finding a worthy subject. Now really spend some time figuring out how to capture it in a way that adds drama, conflict, emotion, or purpose. Most of all, study composition. How can you make use of your subject, its textures and location, to make this image talk to people. Keep shooting, I like your style.', '2006-06-29 01:08:03.000000', NULL, 0),
